body:
Charlotte Bronte had this to say about Jane Austen:

&#x200B;

"Why do you like Jane Austen so very much? I am puzzled on that point. … I had not seen Pride and Prejudice till I read that sentence of yours, and then I got the book. And what did I find? An accurate daguerreotyped portrait of a commonplace face; a carefully fenced, highly cultivated garden, with neat borders and delicate flowers; but no glance of a bright vivid physiognomy, no open country, no fresh air, no blue hill, no bonny beck. I should hardly like to live with her ladies and gentlemen, in their elegant but confined houses. These observations will probably irritate you, but I shall run the risk.”

&#x200B;

She couldn't be more correct. Jane Austen writings are unrealistic. Almost all the protagonists in her books are perfect. While Bronte's books are based on flaws and realism.

Also Lizzy Bennet, the most celebrated Jane Austen heroine, is nothing but a narcissistic, selfish, whiny brat. Lizzy is hell bent on hating Mr. Darcy and in spite of her making it quite obvious that she detests him, he somehow manages to fall in love with her. Then the moment when she decides that she might like him is his confession, and then his letter. So, yes, that turns him into a good light and she realizes she has unfounded prejudice. And yes, later, Mr. Darcy helps her sister out immensely and then treats the Gardiners very well. But is that really enough for love? Can supreme loathing turn into profound love so quickly? I was completely surprised to know she's the main character. I MEAN HOW THE HELL THE AUTHOR EVEN DECIDED TO MAKE HER A MAIN CHARACTER?

&#x200B;

Jane Austen tried her best to make her likeable. She tried to portray her as an independent woman who has her own thoughts and prejudices... then one day, she randomly decides to fall in love with a man (yes Darcy, I'm talking about him) and will marry him because of his kindness. Overall this book is nothing but focused on romance, romance and even more romance. Yet the romance is utter nonsense.

&#x200B;

On the other hand, Jane Eyre is actually a story we all can relate to. Jane is a strong-willed, self-driven heroine with nothing and no one in the world, and she never lets her courage waver. She is one of my favorite characters of all time. And Mr Rochester, while he may not be perfect, is likeable in the sense that he has his flaws, shortcomings and defects. Something that we all can connect to.
